<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    Build policy for the test suites, layered over the repository-wide file.

    The explicit Import is what makes this file a LAYER rather than a replacement, and it is now the file's
    ONLY job. MSBuild stops at the nearest Directory.Build.props on the way up, so without this line the root
    file's TargetFramework, Nullable, TreatWarningsAsErrors, AnalysisLevel and AnalysisMode would all silently
    stop applying to every project under tests/. The file looks redundant and is not; do not delete it.

    This layer used to hold AnalysisMode down at the SDK default while the rest of the repository ran at
    Recommended. It no longer does: the suites inherit Recommended like everything else, and the property is
    stated once, at the root, so the two cannot drift apart.

    What that changed is mostly the CULTURE rules, which the previous version of this comment named as the
    real gap the lowered mode left open. CA1304/CA1305/CA1310/CA1311 arrive with the mode and are opted into
    by name nowhere, so while the suites ran at Default a locale-dependent assertion was caught by review or
    not at all. Their findings were fixed rather than declined. Matching and lookup took
    StringComparison.Ordinal, machine formats took CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, and case folding took the
    invariant overloads.

    Be honest about the yield, because it is what a later reader will want to re-check before deciding the
    mode is worth its noise. MOST of those fixes are inert: an ordinal StartsWith over an ASCII element tag
    matches exactly what the culture-sensitive one matched, and an interpolated int carries no group
    separator in ANY culture — the default format is G, and NumberGroupSeparator is reached only by a format
    string none of these call sites uses. So the counts CatalogTableRenderingTests renders, and the digest
    tables BuiltInCatalogDigestTests regenerates, were already culture-proof and their oracles do not move.

    TWO findings were real, and they are the argument for the mode rather than for opting the four rules in
    by name — neither is the kind of thing review reliably catches, and each was green on every machine this
    repository has ever run on:

      - CopyUtilTests uppercases "alice" and asserts "ALICE". Turkish maps i to the DOTTED capital I (U+0130),
        so ToUpper() there yields ALICE with a dot over the I and the assertion fails. A red test waiting for
        a Turkish machine.
      - SecurityUnitTest parsed "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z" with no provider. DateTimeOffset.Parse resolves a year
        against the CALENDAR of the current culture, so under th-TH (Buddhist) or ar-SA (UmAlQura) the same
        literal is a different date — and the test stays green while asserting the wrong instant.

    The rules the suites DECLINE out of the mode are turned off one at a time, each with its reason, in
    tests/.editorconfig — which is also where the /.globalconfig rules that do not fit test code are turned
    back off. Both halves live there because both are path-scoped decisions about test code; this file is
    about the build, and has no rule opinions of its own.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

</Project>
